***************************** T A L Y S **************************2828 1451  189
                                                                  2828 1451  190
TALYS is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   2828 1451  191
of nuclear reactions. TALYS simulates reactions that involve      2828 1451  192
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    2828 1451  193
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range. This is     2828 1451  194
achieved by implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into  2828 1451  195
a single code system. This nuclear data file is based on a        2828 1451  196
theoretical analysis that utilizes the optical model, compound    2828 1451  197
nucleus statistical theory, fission, direct reactions and         2828 1451  198
pre-equilibrium processes, in combination with databases and      2828 1451  199
models for nuclear structure. The following output of TALYS is    2828 1451  200
stored in this data file:                                         2828 1451  201
                                                                  2828 1451  202
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   2828 1451  203
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     2828 1451  204
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 2828 1451  205
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   2828 1451  206
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                2828 1451  207
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   2828 1451  208
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    2828 1451  209
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        2828 1451  210
- Recoil data                                                     2828 1451  211
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (n,xn), (n,xp),..           2828 1451  212
- Total particle energy spectra                                   2828 1451  213
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      2828 1451  214
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        2828 1451  215
- Residual production cross sections                              2828 1451  216
                                                                  2828 1451  217
For a description of the used nuclear models and their            2828 1451  218
parameterization we refer to [kon19]. If for this specific        2828 1451  219
nuclide we deviate from the global parameterization, it is made   2828 1451  220
explicit above under Nuclide Specific Information.                2828 1451  221
                                                                  2828 1451  222
***************** F I L E   I N F O R M A T I O N ****************2828 1451  223
                                                                  2828 1451  224
##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    2828 1451  225
                                                                  2828 1451  226
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       2828 1451  227
                                                                  2828 1451  228
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          2828 1451  229
                                                                  2828 1451  230
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   2828 1451  231
                                                                  2828 1451  232
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 2828 1451  233
                                                                  2828 1451  234
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     2828 1451  235
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   2828 1451  236
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             2828 1451  237
                                                                  2828 1451  238
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                2828 1451  239
                                                                  2828 1451  240
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       2828 1451  241
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  2828 1451  242
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   2828 1451  243
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  2828 1451  244
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  2828 1451  245
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 2828 1451  246
to the highest incident energy.                                   2828 1451  247
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:2828 1451  248
                                                                  2828 1451  249
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  2828 1451  250
                                                                  2828 1451  251
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    2828 1451  252
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     2828 1451  253
                                                                  2828 1451  254
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     2828 1451  255
                                                                  2828 1451  256
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    2828 1451  257
total cross section.                                              2828 1451  258
                                                                  2828 1451  259
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            2828 1451  260
                                                                  2828 1451  261
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    2828 1451  262
                                                                  2828 1451  263
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        2828 1451  264
                                                                  2828 1451  265
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  2828 1451  266
                                                                  2828 1451  267
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           2828 1451  268
                                                                  2828 1451  269
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   2828 1451  270
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        2828 1451  271
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     2828 1451  272
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         2828 1451  273
sections.                                                         2828 1451  274
                                                                  2828 1451  275
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 2828 1451  276
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 2828 1451  277
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 2828 1451  278
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 2828 1451  279
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      2828 1451  280
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       2828 1451  281
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              2828 1451  282
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  2828 1451  283
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  2828 1451  284
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  2828 1451  285
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  2828 1451  286
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  2828 1451  287
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       2828 1451  288
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        2828 1451  289
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2828 1451  290
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        2828 1451  291
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2828 1451  292
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        2828 1451  293
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2828 1451  294
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        2828 1451  295
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       2828 1451  296
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        2828 1451  297
                                                                  2828 1451  298
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           2828 1451  299
                                                                  2828 1451  300
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    2828 1451  301
discrete levels.                                                  2828 1451  302
                                                                  2828 1451  303
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         2828 1451  304
                                                                  2828 1451  305
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  2828 1451  306
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   2828 1451  307
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      2828 1451  308
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  2828 1451  309
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      2828 1451  310
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         2828 1451  311
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   2828 1451  312
on an angular grid.                                               2828 1451  313
                                                                  2828 1451  314
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       2828 1451  315
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        2828 1451  316
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2828 1451  317
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2828 1451  318
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2828 1451  319
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        2828 1451  320
                                                                  2828 1451  321
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     2828 1451  322
                                                                  2828 1451  323
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     2828 1451  324
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   2828 1451  325
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  2828 1451  326
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             2828 1451  327
All data are generated with TALYS.                                2828 1451  328
                                                                  2828 1451  329
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   2828 1451  330
                                                                  2828 1451  331
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      2828 1451  332
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   2828 1451  333
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   2828 1451  334
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are2828 1451  335
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   2828 1451  336
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  2828 1451  337
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              2828 1451  338
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 2828 1451  339
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  2828 1451  340
For each residual product:                                        2828 1451  341
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               2828 1451  342
- the recoil                                                      2828 1451  343
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         2828 1451  344
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     2828 1451  345
sections.                                                         2828 1451  346
                                                                  2828 1451  347
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     2828 1451  348
                                                                  2828 1451  349
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  2828 1451  350
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 2828 1451  351
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          2828 1451  352
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       2828 1451  353
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 2828 1451  354
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     2828 1451  355
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  2828 1451  356
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      2828 1451  357
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         2828 1451  358
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             2828 1451  359
                                                                  2828 1451  360
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   2828 1451  361
followed:                                                         2828 1451  362
                                                                  2828 1451  363
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2828 1451  364
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2828 1451  365
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2828 1451  366
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     2828 1451  367
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2828 1451  368
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2828 1451  369
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2828 1451  370
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2828 1451  371
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2828 1451  372
                                                                  2828 1451  373
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 2828 1451  374
                                                                  2828 1451  375
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    2828 1451  376
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          2828 1451  377
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     2828 1451  378
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          2828 1451  379
                                                                  2828 1451  380
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  2828 1451  381
                                                                  2828 1451  382
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     2828 1451  383
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    2828 1451  384
as isomer.                                                        2828 1451  385
                                                                  2828 1451  386
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 2828 1451  387
                                                                  2828 1451  388
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        2828 1451  389
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    2828 1451  390
as isomer.                                                        2828 1451  391
                                                                  2828 1451  392
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          2828 1451  393
                                                                  2828 1451  394
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:2828 1451  395
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   2828 1451  396
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   2828 1451  397
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   2828 1451  398
  given                                                           2828 1451  399
                                                                  2828 1451  400
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          2828 1451  401
                                                                  2828 1451  402
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  2828 1451  403
                                                                  2828 1451  404
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 2828 1451  405
                                                                  2828 1451  406
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         2828 1451  407
                                                                  2828 1451  408
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   2828 1451  409
                                                                  2828 1451  410
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        2828 1451  411
and more details are given above in this description. In most     2828 1451  412
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        2828 1451  413
                                                                  2828 1451  414
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 2828 1451  415
                                                                  2828 1451  416
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  2828 1451  417
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     2828 1451  418
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    2828 1451  419
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   2828 1451  420
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  2828 1451  421
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                2828 1451  422
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      2828 1451  423
   same reaction channel between different energies)              2828 1451  424
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      2828 1451  425
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               2828 1451  426
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      2828 1451  427
   different reaction channels for different energies)            2828 1451  428
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        2828 1451  429
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              2828 1451  430
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  2828 1451  431
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       2828 1451  432
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     2828 1451  433
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  2828 1451  434
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     2828 1451  435
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     2828 1451  436
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     2828 1451  437
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   2828 1451  438
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   2828 1451  439
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  2828 1451  440
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   2828 1451  441
                                                                  2828 1451  442
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  2828 1451  443
                                                                  2828 1451  444
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       2828 1451  445
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  2828 1451  446
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       2828 1451  447
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   2828 1451  448
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         2828 1451  449
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  2828 1451  450
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      2828 1451  451
representation is used.                                           2828 1451  452
                                                                  2828 1451  453
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      2828 1451  454
                                                                  2828 1451  455
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  2828 1451  456
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             2828 1451  457
                                                                  2828 1451  458
